name: bits
intro: A byte is eight on/off switches. Hardware registers, file modes,
intro: network headers and flags all pack many answers into one number, and
intro: C gives you the operators to reach in:
intro:
intro:     a & b    1 where both are 1       (mask: keep these bits)
intro:     a | b    1 where either is 1      (set bits)
intro:     a ^ b    1 where they differ      (toggle bits)
intro:     ~a       flip every bit
intro:     a << n   move bits up n places    (multiply by 2^n)
intro:     a >> n   move bits down n places  (divide by 2^n)
intro:
intro: The four moves you will use forever:
intro:
intro:     x |= 1u << 3;      /* set bit 3 */
intro:     x &= ~(1u << 3);   /* clear bit 3 */
intro:     x ^= 1u << 3;      /* toggle bit 3 */
intro:     if (x & (1u << 3)) /* test bit 3 */
intro:
intro: Do bit work on unsigned types, from <stdint.h>: `uint8_t`,
intro: `uint32_t`. Shifting a negative signed number, shifting a signed
intro: value into its sign bit, or shifting by the width of the type or
intro: more (`x << 32` on a 32-bit value) are all undefined behaviour --
intro: the compiler may do anything, and it changes between machines and
intro: optimisation levels. Unsigned `>>` always fills with zeros; signed
intro: `>>` of a negative is up to the compiler.
intro:
intro: Small types promote: `uint8_t b = 0x80; ~b` is an int with its high
intro: bits set, not 0x7f. Cast back, or mask with `& 0xffu`.
intro:
intro: Print hex with `%x` (`%02x` pads to two digits). For `uint32_t`
intro: portably use `PRIx32` from <inttypes.h>:
intro:
intro:     printf("%08" PRIx32 "\n", v);
intro:
intro: Parsing a number from text for bit work: `strtoul` quietly accepts
intro: `-1` and wraps it to a huge value. Check the first character is a
intro: digit yourself, then check the end pointer, `errno` and the range.
---
q: popcnt.c -- count the one bits
spec: Take one or more arguments, each an unsigned decimal number from 0
spec: to 4294967295 (digits only -- no sign, no spaces). For each, in
spec: order, print on its own line how many of its 32 bits are 1.
spec:
spec: At the first argument that is not such a number, print
spec: `popcnt: bad number: ARG` to standard error and exit 1; lines already
spec: printed stay. With no arguments print `usage: popcnt N...` to
spec: standard error and exit 2.
file: popcnt.c
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <stdint.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: 
prog: static int popcount32(uint32_t x)
prog: {
prog: 	int n = 0;
prog: 	for (; x != 0; x >>= 1)
prog: 		n += (int)(x & 1u);
prog: 	return n;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: static int parse_u32(const char *s, uint32_t *out)
prog: {
prog: 	if (s[0] < '0' || s[0] > '9')
prog: 		return -1;
prog: 	char *end;
prog: 	errno = 0;
prog: 	unsigned long v = strtoul(s, &end, 10);
prog: 	if (*end != '\0' || errno != 0 || v > UINT32_MAX)
prog: 		return -1;
prog: 	*out = (uint32_t)v;
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char **argv)
prog: {
prog: 	if (argc < 2) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "usage: popcnt N...\n");
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 	}
prog: 	for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
prog: 		uint32_t v;
prog: 		if (parse_u32(argv[i], &v) != 0) {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "popcnt: bad number: %s\n", argv[i]);
prog: 			return 1;
prog: 		}
prog: 		printf("%d\n", popcount32(v));
prog: 	}
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: popcnt 0 1 255 4294967295
case: popcnt 7 x 9; echo $?
case: popcnt -1; echo $?
case: popcnt 4294967296; echo $?
case: popcnt; echo $?
hint: x & 1 is the lowest bit; shift right until the number is empty -- and strtoul will happily accept a minus sign, so look at the first character yourself
